and trying to find the right--

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.340
I think right now we're
just looking at tools

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.340
and focused on that.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:02.600
- Yeah.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.170
How do you approach that like
knowing what to focus on first?

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.410
- So far, my approach has been
to have discussions with our HR managers.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:17.290
So those are the primary
stakeholders are the ones that

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:20.470
are going to be
leveraging the data

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:25.120
and communicating it to either
higher up or to the different

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.820
managers within
the organization.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.630
And also thinking about
security, and because with HR

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.970
data, it's highly

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:35.890
secure

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.300
information that we share.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.490
We don't always want
to share demographics.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:41.920
So also thinking about

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.920
security policies.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.740
But just a lot of--

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.480
yeah, just a lot of
conversations with people

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.790
about what their
business needs are.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.910
- Definitely. I see--

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.880
I'm just looking over to the
chat here, too. I see Eugene,

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.890
you had a few thoughts
on this topic.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:02.890
Do you want to jump in here?

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.770
- Sure. I'll say, hi.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.590
I had to build a team

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:15.110
at a previous position.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.450
I'm currently a team
of one and, hopefully,

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.680
will be building a team soon.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.160
I think

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.410
there are very few unicorns,

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.740
which I put three
things up there like--

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.190
there's a use case
and a necessity.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:32.720
Sometimes,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.480
you need somebody to be able
to build things as fast as possible.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.730
So you've got to go for a
programmer or people who really

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.890
programming, who can
build a pipeline,

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:43.470
who feel they
understand production level stuff,

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.520
have dabbled in multiple
tools, et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.330
I can't necessarily rely on somebody
like that to be the math genius,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.850
someone who understands
integration and probability

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:56.210
distributions and difference
between a poisson and an

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:59.990
exponential and la, la, la.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.660
That person also
has a lot of value,

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.270
and everyone can learn
from them, too. And sometimes,

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.420
if you're in a place where
you need very sophisticated

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:12.530
modeling to take a leap up,

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.330
you need a person
like that probably,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:17.000
who can bring in
that extra skill set.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.430
And then there's the
contextual experts.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:24.300
And I'm currently in a position
where I am not a contextual expert.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.220
I am in bioinformatics,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.370
which is my first foray into
the medical field of data. It is--

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.740
the data is more disgusting
in the medical field than pretty

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.620
much anywhere I've
ever seen, by the way.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.150
But the

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.500
contextual expert probably in this
role is more important than anyone,

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:45.440
because they're going to be
spending a lot of time trying

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.450
to figure out, is
this a generic drug?

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:51.710
Or is this a name of a brand?
Or what is an encounter?

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.560
Why is this all
event level data

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.170
and not something where it's at
a patient level where I can do

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.980
my analytics? How do I
convert things from patient level to--

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.640
or from event level
to patient level?

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.870
A contextual expert
would be very,

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:10.250
very probably the most useful person
I would dive in on if I

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.940
had to hire someone in
a data science position

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.530
underneath me

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:17.190
in a team in bioinformatics.

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:20.090
So to me, the first
question is really like,

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:23.690
what are going to
be the highest impact projects?

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:28.430
And who of those three major
skill sets would I dive in on

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:33.500
to try and at least
create a my first employee

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.970
and then round out based
on their weaknesses? But I would

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:40.309
aim for trying to cover
those three bases.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:42.780
If you're really going to build
a whole team of four or five,

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.920
six people, you really need
people who are, in my opinion,

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.945
really good experts at each--

00:08:47.945 --> 00:08:49.550
at one of those three things,

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.070
because it's very rare to find
somebody who's an expert in all

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:54.530
three. And you'd be paying out

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:57.500
a ton of money if you do
find someone who's an expert in all three.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:00.410
-

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:03.320
I think that kind of
answers your question you had asked

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:09.980
Libby, too. But do you want to ask
that to a few others here in the group?

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.080
- Sure. Yeah. That's the

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.330
experience that I have with

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:16.760
talking with people and
interviewing people,

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:18.890
interviewing with people,

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:20.610
is that when they
are building a team,

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.700
they're looking for somebody
to hit the ground running.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:23.810
And they always use that term,

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.340
we need somebody to
hit the ground running.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.290
And a lot of times what I
feel like they're saying is we need

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.670
somebody to do everything.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.240
And we also need somebody
to tell us what to do,

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.160
because we don't
know what to do.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.350
And that feels really scary,

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:38.600
because I often think,
well, I'm not that person.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:40.700
I'm a stats person. I'm
applied stats person.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.000
That's my side of the game.
I can't be everything for

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.450
everybody. So as a person who

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.520
is looking at the industry
that freaks me out,

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.230
it sounds like you guys are
also saying that person doesn't really exist.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.990
And my

00:09:56.990 --> 00:09:59.510
take on that is if
you have somebody who does everything

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:00.350
and knows everything,

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.230
they are now your single point
of failure. And if they leave,

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.550
something happens to them,

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.520
all of your expertise
goes in one person.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.100
And instead of needing to
replace the one person who was

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.560
maybe a SME or maybe
the programming person,

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.850
you now have nothing. So
I'm curious about people's thoughts on

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.560
building a team

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.080
of people who are

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.570
lower level and
not those unicorns.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:27.080
And how do you make that
work, what do you look for?

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:27.440
KATHERINE: Libby,

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:31.320
I can't figure out how to raise
my hand right now in Zoom.

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.810
- That's OK.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.350
- I probably should
say that too. Yeah.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:40.370
So if anybody looks at the
bottom where it says reactions,

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:41.780
there's like a
little Smiley face.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:42.890
KATHERINE: Oh,
there it is. Yeah.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.930
- Sorry, I should have put
that in there. But thanks,

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:46.340
Katherine, for jumping in.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.220
KATHERINE: I was in full screen
and so that had gone away.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.780
I feel like a lot of times,

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:53.840
looking for people who can
hit the ground running is like

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.050
corporate code for we are not going
to onboard you or train you at all.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:10:59.120
And

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:02.960
that's really something that I
think when you are building a

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:06.200
data science team, like

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.180
they were talking about
before, you need to say,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.270
these are the people that I
need and the skill sets that

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:15.410
they're going to have and
then build your onboarding around

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.060
what that team is
going to look like.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.260
One of the things that we've
been doing is trying to get

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:25.290
away from, oh, well, this
one person knows everything.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:26.870
And so like for six months,

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.540
all we did was write
documentation about our

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:34.100
processes and transcribe email
chains from three years ago

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:37.580
into things like that so that
when we do bring on new people,

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:42.300
we can onboard them not only
on the technical aspect of the job,

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:45.440
but also on the context
of the data especially because we're

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:48.650
in the medical field as well
where you are bringing on

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.515
people who may not have had
any medical experience before.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.680
- Definitely.
Thank you all for--

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:01.240
I'm trying to read through the
chat here for people who want

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.265
to comment on a
specific topic too.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:11.350
Coulter, I see that you had
shared when I first said about

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.590
starting the first data
science team that you started

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:18.970
a data team. And I'm curious,
did you have to build

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.900
up the business case for that
or were you hired on to start

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:25.912
the data science team?

00:12:25.912 --> 00:12:29.360
COULTER: Yeah. Hey, everyone.
Kind of almost both.

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.360
So I work for a local
government public health

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.760
department. And

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.550
it was essentially uncharted territory
when it comes to data

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:39.080
science. Everything is--

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:44.120
it was done in Excel. And I
think I kind of tried to get

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.580
buy-in on this from a selfish
point of view because I was

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.170
doing all this data
and I was like,

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:50.540
this shouldn't take this long.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.590
We're spending a ton of
time trying to do all this.

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:57.200
So luckily, I had great
leadership that said, you know

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.770
what, that's right, we
should start something

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.960
and

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:07.040
was able to hire someone
and immediately just trying to

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.890
convert everything to R,
automate as many things as

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.200
possible. And I love--

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:13.640
I guess I'll preface
the next part--

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:19.160
I love hand-washing dishes because
the catharsis from it. And

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:23.120
the first time that we
were able to show someone, hey,

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:25.280
you know this thing that
used to take you 16 hours,

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.920
it takes 30 seconds now.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.490
And the joy in their face--

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.350
I will never forget that.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:34.340
And I think that really
showed leadership.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:38.630
This is something worth
investing in even further.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:40.880
So that was about 2
and 1/2 years ago.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.640
And like I said, we were
able to hire one person to join.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:49.130
And now we are a team of 8.
And it's been super awesome.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:50.720
Everything, everybody's
already said,

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.010
has been bits and pieces in
our experience as well either from

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:59.210
security or just scaling, just
things in general. So it's been

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:03.470
fun. Still got a long way to
go, a lot of cool stuff to learn.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:09.470
- That's awesome. You go from starting
the team to eight now. How did you

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.350
figure out what those
roles should be,

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.460
those eight people?

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.600
COULTER: Oh, great question. I
originally kind of designed it--

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.010
and someone had just
mentioned this--

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:20.100
as hey,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:24.080
this person may be
focuses on specifically visualizations,

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.590
maybe pipelines that get to
a visualization, and then

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:30.410
realize, for continuity
of operation purposes,

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:33.300
that wasn't going
to be feasible.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:37.400
So switch to a different method
of almost everybody is kind of

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:40.190
trained on everything and can
kind of do stuff if needed.

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:46.130
But no one is really expected to
be an expert at every other person's job

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.940
because that's asking
a lot from people and

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:51.260
as a leader,

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:53.510
I don't think anyone should
expect every person on the team

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:57.510
to be the expert at everything.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.830
- Absolutely. I wanted to
remind everybody to, if you

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:05.370
feel OK or comfortable with
it, just introducing yourself too,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:09.690
especially for people who are joining us
for the first time here as well.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:15.600
OK, let me double check.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.870
And Curtis and Hannah are
helping me organize questions.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:23.410
But anybody else want to
comment on this topic too?

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.410
Oh, Alan, I see your hand.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.820
Did we lose Alan?

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.820
Alan, can you hear us?

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:38.120
- I can see you talking,
Alan. But no sound.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.250
