00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:51.390
Anybody else have thoughts on this,
building up your first data team?

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:52.320
- Is that better?

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.007
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Yep, there you are.

00:15:54.007 --> 00:15:56.007
- OK.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.260
You turn on your computer
one day and it's like,

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.480
I don't have a
microphone. [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.480
is a microphone.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.690
What was I going to talk about?

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:10.740
I think so sort of working
on the whole sort of circuit from

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.630
what Libby was
saying to [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.370
Katherine to Coulter--

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:15.180
I was thinking about it.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.820
I think I've had a really

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:21.270
interesting experience
with my team of

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:24.210
saying for a number of years,

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:28.080
we need cross supportability for
the things that we're doing

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.160
and finding that as the
team grows, just the demand grows.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.130
We have more stakeholder teams
that we're connected with and

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:39.120
people end up being kind of
de facto kind of drawn with dotted

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:41.820
lines to those stakeholder
teams and getting kind of

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:45.900
specialized in those lanes versus

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:48.030
being able to sort of
share and cross support.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.710
And that's been a
real challenge because

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.580
for years, I've wanted to help

00:16:56.580 --> 00:17:01.870
certain folks who support really high
intensity stuff to have more help.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.849
And I think probably I've had
a combination of really good luck

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:10.500
getting good people on the team
who come in and are eager to

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:11.819
help to do that.

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:16.589
But I think we hit a little bit
of a critical mass with the team where

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:18.480
there were enough people--

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.310
and the team is now 6--

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:25.200
and I have enough people who
are curious and who are skilled

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:30.030
who can help without getting
sort of completely pulled into

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:34.770
the pool of being
able to cross support.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:40.180
And so whether it's
disciplinary or subject matter expertise,

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:42.030
I really have felt
like in the last year,

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:46.590
there's been a tipping point of
my team is big enough now that

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:51.240
they can help one another whereas
when it was two, they were just

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.220
head down focused on
getting stuff done.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:54.720
And then when it was four,

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:57.990
there was a little more
room but still pretty head down.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:59.130
And now with six,

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:02.790
there's enough of a cohesive
team there that they can share,

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:05.310
they can collaborate without
me driving all of the

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.930
collaboration.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.600
It feels like that size has
really opened up a bunch of new

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.860
opportunity that I don't think
I could have created with two

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.200
or three or four people.

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:19.140
And it's got a whole new set of

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.220
working

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:21.540
and meeting challenges.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:25.440
But their ability to run has
really taken off in such a cool

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:28.860
way in the last six months as
they've kind of got up to speed

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.000
with one another.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.240
So I don't know
if that leads to

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.330
a concluding point,

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:38.820
but it's been really neat
to see that transition over time

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:40.620
as the team has grown.
And they've gotten to know one

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.570
another, develop their skills,

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:45.520
and develop their
subject matter expertise.

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:46.180
It's been really,

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:49.230
really fun and rewarding to see
them get good at what they do

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:53.200
and get really good at
helping one another. It's really cool.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.420
- That's great.
Thank you, Alan.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.060
Katherine, I see your hand
raised. Do you want to jump in?

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:03.130
KATHERINE: Yeah, I love that
from Alan because that sort of

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:09.250
collaborative work really facilitates the
rest of the data science.

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:12.730
It's something that I've
struggled with is making sure

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:17.470
that people get credit for
doing that where it frequently becomes

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:19.870
unseen work. You're not
emailing a spreadsheet to

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:22.000
someone, you're not

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.630
doing that, but fostering
that relationship,

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:28.690
being active in your chat,

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:30.820
sending out those
emails to those teams,

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:34.660
building a business use
case around something

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:39.700
is so important to staying connected
to the rest of those teams.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:43.630
And so we have a strategy
for getting credit for that.

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:47.200
But if anyone else has
anything where how do you, one,

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:50.440
manage that unseen work but
also make sure that the people

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:53.180
who are doing it still
get credit for it,

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.180
I'd love to hear about that.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:03.500
- Can I ask you, Katherine,
what's your team strategy for that?

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.440
KATHERINE: We do something
every quarter called

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:09.890
Take a Bow where we basically
have our members write up,

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:12.230
and they do it in
an hour markdown,

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:16.460
but they write up everything
that they think that they did

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:18.560
throughout the quarter.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:22.640
And then they send that to
me and my sister team manager. And

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:26.150
we aggregate that information
and we escalate it up our

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.140
reporting pipeline.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:31.490
So it's got the hard details
of the stuff that we did--

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:32.660
here's the GitLab repo,

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:36.980
here's the business value that
this created, et cetera. But also,

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:41.330
I did all of these other
things like I was active on my

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:42.980
committee board--

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:46.190
anything else that you're a part
of that want to make sure

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:48.440
that you get credit for,
you can escalate that.

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:52.130
So I did kind of like
outsource the management of that credit.

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:56.720
But it still makes sure that
it's at least seen by like

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:00.840
one and two levels above
the person who's doing that work.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:03.470
But it's a lot of manual stuff.

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:05.670
- Yeah.

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:08.460
Somebody had
recommended to me in the

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:13.110
R Ladies Slack Group to
like every week, send yourself an

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:17.700
email of all of the things that
you did that week. So then at

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:21.210
the end of the year or
whenever you're showing

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:23.130
your leadership
team what you did,

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:25.060
you remember all
those little things.

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:29.430
And I think a lot of what
I do is sometimes that glue work

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:31.770
that I sometimes
forget like, oh,

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:35.010
that was really important that
I did that and I connected

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:36.970
these teams across the company.

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:40.610
But if I didn't write it
down for myself, I wouldn't

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:43.550
maybe have remembered it or
thought it was as important.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:51.080
Any other thoughts on what
other teams are doing to kind

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.080
of highlight some of that work?

00:21:59.360 --> 00:22:01.360
I see Aaron.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:04.790
You had a comment
in the chat here.

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:07.240
Oh, yeah, around
annual review time.

00:22:13.984 --> 00:22:17.280
OK, Mark, I'm curious,
what's your team's process?

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:24.910
- Sorry, I was on mute.

00:22:24.910 --> 00:22:27.370
Yeah, Aaron said

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:30.790
in the chat that she
