00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:31.600
My team--

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:36.340
I work for a health care system
in Houston on a group of analysts.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.070
We're really small. We have like
four of us on the team.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:40.390
So we're really tight knit,

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:43.900
but we all have our
little domain area of expertise.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:46.430
But we all do
something similar.

00:22:46.430 --> 00:22:49.600
The only difference is we
all share that same one note

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.970
that our manager
sees and we keep

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:54.380
track of with
weekly one on ones.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:57.520
So it's a really good way
to just see across the year what

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.640
you're working on and then look
back and see what you've accomplished.

00:23:03.830 --> 00:23:06.350
- Definitely. I see

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:09.420
Jeffrey had asked a great
question in the chat.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.300
I know you don't have a mic
at the moment. So I'll read it.

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:14.000
But the question was

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:18.800
at what point and how do
you decide to stop focusing on the

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:22.580
non-analytics work that has
filled your backlog and push

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:25.460
for more of the
advanced analytics.

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.430
A lot of our issue is
requests come in and they are dashboards

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:32.030
that should be done by more
of a business analyst and not a

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:34.940
data engineer or data
scientist. But for us,

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:38.465
it's difficult to dig out of
that hole or find a stopping point.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:46.400
I see a few
comments here in the

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:49.190
thread of that. So I'll
start with you, Jared,

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:51.740
if you feel like
jumping in here.

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:56.150
And sorry if I'm--

00:23:56.150 --> 00:23:56.840
JARED: Hey, Rachel.
Can you hear me?

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:58.190
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Oh,
there you are. Yes.

00:23:58.190 --> 00:23:59.520
JARED: Sorry about that.

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:00.590
- No worries.

00:24:00.590 --> 00:24:01.250
JARED: Yeah, no,

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:04.820
I think it's just kind of
like he was talking about further

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:07.010
down like chicken
or egg. If you're

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:09.140
getting to the point where

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:14.810
the workload in the backlog is
just becoming insurmountable, how do you

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:18.860
do a more efficient job of

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:21.440
organizing and kind of
disseminating that to push the

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:23.510
needle forward instead of

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:28.070
just kind of being drowned out
of the work? So do you take

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:31.340
the approach of trying to

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:35.750
split the work and split the
idea of having a focus on

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:39.800
analytics versus
non-analytics teams or

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:44.360
are you trying to hire more
of those unicorns that can do both

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:47.780
sides of that to help and

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:49.700
how do you manage

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:51.890
your approach that?

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:54.358
I think that's the big question
for me anyway. [LAUGHS]

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:56.740
-

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:00.430
I thought something that was
interesting on the topic of

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:02.695
dashboards is I think it was--

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:08.430
I'm blanking on
who had said this--

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:12.080
but if you stopped creating
any of those dashboards,

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.570
which ones would
people really notice

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:18.170
that were missing and which
ones are we just creating for

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:21.290
the sake of creating
them and updating them.

00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:23.750
KATHERINE:

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:27.410
I was remembering that too from
a couple of weeks ago and even

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:30.560
going back to Libby's comment
in the chat of like what's

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:33.900
going to happen if
you don't do that.

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.870
And if the answer is

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:38.810
$4 billion of critical
business processes stop,

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:41.960
then they probably need to
hire someone to make that dashboard.

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.030
And if the answer is one

00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:47.300
guy gets a little upset,
then maybe you don't make it.

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:51.790
- Exactly.

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:53.200
Jonathan,

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:55.630
Remmers, or Chris,

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:57.680
I see you had some
thoughts on this topic too.

00:25:57.680 --> 00:25:59.582
Do you want to jump in here?

00:25:59.582 --> 00:26:01.582
Let see if anybody unmutes.

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:13.070
- I was saying that I
get frustrated because

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.530
descriptive analytics
has a bad name now.

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:17.930
Nobody wants to come into
our field to do descriptive

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:20.990
analytics or reporting. But
it's actually still very important.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:24.620
And

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:26.210
you just can
encourage yourself.

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:30.620
If the descriptive stuff you're
doing is very important, it's OK.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:33.750
And it's not always easy
to put together either.

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:36.660
But if you're trying
to make that switch,

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:39.800
you may have to first
convince people of the value.

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:41.720
Not just say, oh, we want
to do advanced analytics.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.220
They don't understand
what that means.

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:46.520
But what's the outcome
that you want to achieve

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:50.060
that you need to go do
advanced analytics to get to.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:53.660
And some of what I've
done internally is talking to

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:56.280
stakeholders in the company
what are their goals,

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:57.740
what do they want to know,

00:26:57.740 --> 00:26:59.510
what are questions that
they haven't asked,

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:01.490
what are questions they

00:27:01.490 --> 00:27:03.500
don't ask because

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:06.650
they don't want to be
disappointed that there's not an answer.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:08.650
So I will ask people to

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.670
think without any restrictions.

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:15.870
Don't worry about what
we can or can't do.

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:17.540
What would you like to see?

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.990
And then if you can get people
who would sponsor, yeah,

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:22.580
these are the outcomes
we would like to get,

00:27:22.580 --> 00:27:25.310
then you have a little
more weight behind that request to

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:28.970
try to shift your time away
from descriptive analytics to something else.

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:32.890
- Thank you.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.080
Gerard, I see you have
your hand raised here too.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:36.760
You want to jump
in on this topic?

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:39.010
- Yeah, I think what

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:42.190
you just said is
critical because

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:46.480
getting access to the data is usually
one of the harder things to do.

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:50.740
So if you have an executive who
wants a report or a dashboard or whatever,

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:52.810
you can break that

00:27:52.810 --> 00:27:57.400
deadlock in getting access to the
data. And once you have it,

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:01.360
you can do the more advanced
analytics and start to build that

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:05.480
business case of if we look at
it this way, if we do more,

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:07.280
we can give you more value.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.800
But in the end,

00:28:08.800 --> 00:28:13.660
getting that data and breaking up
that process of getting the data,

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:16.960
processing it and analyzing
it in three different steps--

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:19.310
it helps in a
variety of things.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:22.450
It helps in building your
team slowly because those are

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:26.140
sometimes three different expertises.
And you can potentially

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:33.670
give the getting the data to IT so
that you don't have to do it. So

00:28:33.670 --> 00:28:34.920
it

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:38.340
opens up time for

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:41.710
your own analysis.

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:44.850
It also makes it easier to go
all the way to the beginning of

00:28:44.850 --> 00:28:48.250
the discussion about
hitting the ground running.

00:28:48.250 --> 00:28:50.100
You have

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:52.870
people who are good at these
three different things.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:55.500
And I think when they describe

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:57.420
that they're looking for people
who are hitting the ground

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:01.410
running, are people that
can answer that final question,

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:05.750
that give that report to
the executive and give

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:08.190
that final result.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:10.730
So if you can use that

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:13.715
from a report, from a
straightforward dashboard,

00:29:13.715 --> 00:29:17.810
if you can use that
to break the deadlocking and getting

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:22.130
access to the data and
start slowly building some analytics,

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:24.380
I think that's a
good way to start.

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:27.560
You have to be
very careful that,

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.660
to also what
people were saying,

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:32.630
that it doesn't become your
day job and your only day job.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:36.205
-

00:29:36.205 --> 00:29:40.210
Can I just confirm with anybody
here what is descriptive

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:43.465
analytics exactly mean

00:29:43.465 --> 00:29:47.318
or advanced analytics?

00:29:47.318 --> 00:29:49.950
KATHERINE: I kind of view--

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:54.150
- I was thinking that there's
this classic three or four step thing.

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:58.920
It's like you have descriptive
and then diagnostic and then

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:00.750
predictive and
then prescriptive.

00:30:00.750 --> 00:30:01.920
That was like
something maybe

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.080
8, 10 years ago
that made the rounds.

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.660
KATHERINE: I've seen
that too, Chris.

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:08.160
- I think people, when
they say analytics,

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:10.560
they're talking about
everything to the right of

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:13.230
descriptive on that visual.

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:14.130
- OK.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:17.340
- Descriptive is like what
is going on right now,

00:30:17.340 --> 00:30:20.010
how many of something.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:24.630
KATHERINE: --this is what
is happening or has happened.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:27.300
But diagnostic is kind of
like what has happened.

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:30.390
I think it's just, yeah,
this is what it is.

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:31.470
- Cool. Thank you.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:33.780
KATHERINE: And you can make
a bar graph of, I think,

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:37.200
counts as descriptive
analytics.

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:39.210
- Ralph, I see your
hands raised here.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:39.630
JARED: By the way,

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:43.620
you just can't do any of
those other three things good at all

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:47.340
if you don't do
descriptive analytics.

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:47.520
-

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:49.770
What I found with working
with clients is helpful is I

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:53.310
actually made an image of
a pyramid where the first layer

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:56.040
is descriptive analytics, and
then predictive is smaller,

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.120
and then prescriptive
is on the top.

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:00.930
And so with the idea that
majority of your time running

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:04.500
your business and most of your
efforts as a data team should

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:07.380
be in that descriptive side
because that's the basis of

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.470
anything to predict the future
or making large changes in your

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:12.250
business. So that's
been helpful.

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:15.180
And then I also started
adding a basement of data management.

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:16.080
- Yeah, I was going to say,

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.200
there's a data quality
layer under there somewhere.

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:19.275
-

00:31:19.275 --> 00:31:23.840
I call it the basement even though
I don't know if pyramids have basements.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:28.160
- That's great. George,
I see your hands raised.

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:31.460
- Oh, yeah. I just wanted
to chime in on the descriptive.

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:33.890
Yeah, to kind of reinforce
what was said about--

00:31:33.890 --> 00:31:36.380
it's just as important

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.170
and crucial and fundamental.
And it made me think of--

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:44.390
actually, this morning, I cracked
open my Edward Tufte book

00:31:44.390 --> 00:31:46.370
and just

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:49.860
in awe over the descriptive
stats that are in there.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:52.700
But the way that
they're presented--

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:54.200
they're presented

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:57.620
in a way that solves a
problem. There's the classic

00:31:57.620 --> 00:32:00.020
cholera outbreak

00:32:00.020 --> 00:32:01.850
map that was done.

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:05.190
And it's like,
that's descriptive analytics,

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:07.610
but it's just in a way
that's telling a story.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:10.070
It's in a way that
resonates with people.

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:13.940
So it's not necessarily
like, oh, it's just so simple

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.850
and maybe not

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:20.360
worth our time.
But it's more like

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:23.880
how do you convey those
descriptive stats.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:25.760
It could be as simple
as a bar chart.

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:29.150
But is that really
answering the question,

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:31.370
is that really getting to--

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:33.450
it could be it could
be descriptive stats.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:38.720
That's all they need. But
again, how it's conveyed is

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:40.720
vital.

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.320
- Definitely. Thank you.

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:46.660
I'm loving this
lively discussion here. Mike,

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:48.510
I see your hand raised.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:49.290
- Hi, yeah.

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:52.710
I was just going to say that
I heard a great talk from

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:56.160
a data science leader I think
working for one of the big

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:59.220
supermarkets in the UK who
describe descriptive analytics

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.630
as kind of looking in
the rear view mirror.

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:04.660
So what has happened.

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:06.460
So maybe that's

00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:09.530
also quite a good analogy.

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:16.780
- So do people generally use
BI tools for more of descriptive analytics

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:18.430
and predictive

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:21.910
or whatever is past
that is more of--

00:33:21.910 --> 00:33:21.970
KATHERINE:

00:33:21.970 --> 00:33:25.180
I still use orange ggplot
plot for all of my descriptive

00:33:25.180 --> 00:33:28.015
analytics. But Power BI
is really powerful too.

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:36.190
JARED: They will usually
rely on business analysts

00:33:36.190 --> 00:33:38.590
to produce dashboards
of descriptive statistics.

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:41.470
But it doesn't necessarily ever
imply that you're going to get

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:47.860
that extra layer of context as to
maybe why those stories are taking place.

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:55.470
- I see on the topic
of like getting rid of some dashboards,

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:57.180
Mark, you had a great
question earlier.

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:59.180
Do you want to jump in here?

00:34:01.630 --> 00:34:02.970
- I think he had to run out.

00:34:02.970 --> 00:34:05.770
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Oh, OK. Well,

00:34:05.770 --> 00:34:06.460
the question was,

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:09.900
does anyone have experience
or suggestions for getting folks

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:12.810
to agree to
deleting dashboards.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:14.850
Mark said, our group
has so many dashboards.

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:18.719
We maintain with only slightly different
views of the same data set.

00:34:18.719 --> 00:34:22.380
And I have thus far been
unable to get buy-in even though the

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.989
technical debt we've
incurred is astounding.

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:29.880
- I have a few
comments on this.

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.320
So I'm at Centene

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:36.870
along with Katherine

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:39.870
who's talked a
bit this morning.

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:43.710
We have 75,000 employees or so.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:49.170
And I actually just helped
run a Hackathon for anyone that

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:51.499
wanted to participate
at the company. And

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:54.389
the whole purpose,

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:58.560
the whole problem statement
was effectively what can we do

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.710
about our dashboard
craze situation.

00:35:01.710 --> 00:35:04.230
And believe it or not,

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:08.160
outside of this Hackathon,
since January 1st,

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:12.940
there have been about 25,000
dashboards that have been deleted.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:16.530
So the idea is

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:21.210
what is going on with so
many dashboards and reports that are

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:24.000
now all on the cloud.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:25.780
And so

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:30.940
yeah, it's a serious problem.
And I don't think that,

00:35:30.940 --> 00:35:33.370
for larger
corporations at least,

00:35:33.370 --> 00:35:37.900
I kind of feel like the
thought has been just create a

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:41.410
dashboard, if it's not used,
don't even really think about it.

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:49.350
Yeah, if we need to delete this
later, we can, no big deal.

00:35:49.350 --> 00:35:52.290
But there's costs associated with
all this and the resources

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:56.930
that are used to just
keep those online.

00:35:56.930 --> 00:35:58.700
So yeah,

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:03.110
this whole Hackathon was done to
really try and drill in on

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:05.900
can we put some strategy behind

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:09.530
a rationalization approach or
some kind of decommissioning

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:12.740
approach or strategy
to all these different

00:36:14.780 --> 00:36:18.020
reports and dashboards
that the company has. And

00:36:18.020 --> 00:36:19.520
I'm not just talking--

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:24.860
25,000 shiny apps would be
incredible. I'm talking more like

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:25.910
reports

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:28.310
and dashboards across
all of our platforms--

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:32.970
Power BI, Tableau,
MicroStrategy, et cetera.

00:36:32.970 --> 00:36:35.580
- The Hackathon sounds
really cool too.

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:38.070
- Yeah, the winners
did an excellent job,

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:43.710
coming out with kind of a
solution that had more of that prescriptive

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:46.900
insights for leadership.

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.410
So I'm really excited to
see what we do about maybe

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:52.380
productionizing that as kind of
a Shiny app or something like

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:58.260
this for leadership to have
a clear visibility into how

00:36:58.260 --> 00:37:00.630
many reports should

00:37:00.630 --> 00:37:03.180
be on a decommissioning path or

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:05.700
et cetera. Yeah.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:06.120
- [? Annas, ?]

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:08.620
I see your hands raised.
Do you want to jump in?

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:11.342
- Sure. So I guess
one gentle way to

00:37:13.500 --> 00:37:18.930
test the waters for governance for
some of these dashboards is to--

00:37:18.930 --> 00:37:23.490
if it gets updated on a
constant basis, try one period

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:26.010
to not update it or one month
not to update it and see what

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:28.560
reactions you get. And
if it's a live feed,

00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:31.650
try to cut it off and see
what reactions you get.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:34.620
Now if no one says anything
and they're not really paying

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.900
attention to it, then that's a
good sign. And you can say, OK,

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:40.410
this is probably not being used

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:43.710
as much as it should be
or as much as expected.

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:47.100
But obviously, if
you hear people

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:50.340
express their dissent and
say, no, we rely on this data,

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:52.980
then that's a good indicator to
say that the dashboard is still

00:37:52.980 --> 00:37:54.980
relevant and

00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:59.475
the stakeholders still need
it to be in place and active.

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:05.080
- Thank you.

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:06.340
I see

00:38:06.340 --> 00:38:08.540
Abigail, you have your
hand raised here too.

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:10.210
You want to jump in next?

00:38:10.210 --> 00:38:12.250
- Yeah. So we monitor

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:16.240
our usage of dashboards. I
mean, that's really the start.

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:18.160
Tableau was APIs--

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:19.610
I don't know what
other tools have.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:22.270
But you can see who's
looking at your dashboards and when.

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:24.460
And so being able to go in
with like nobody's looked at this in

00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:26.980
three months, that's a
really strong argument--

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:29.650
literally nobody's using
this. And even then,

00:38:29.650 --> 00:38:31.330
nobody wanted anything deleted.

00:38:31.330 --> 00:38:32.380
But

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:35.530
we were able to
get permission to

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:38.590
basically hide dashboards
nobody was using except for the

00:38:38.590 --> 00:38:40.510
people who we're
actually building the dashboards,

00:38:40.510 --> 00:38:44.080
write some documentation that says
what all the ones that are

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:45.790
in hibernation do.
So in the future,

00:38:45.790 --> 00:38:47.830
somebody wants to bring it
back, they can read that and

00:38:47.830 --> 00:38:49.210
say, oh, I needed this.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:51.160
But it's not something that
we're having to worry about

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:54.620
maintaining on a regular basis.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:56.080
- Do you think sometimes,

00:38:56.080 --> 00:39:00.130
it's that the dashboard
or whatever visualization was

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:02.800
really helpful for someone
when they first saw it,

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:06.420
but then they don't know
where to ever find it again?

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:08.050
- No, because nobody's
looked at it in three months.

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:10.080
I think people just really get
freaked out by the idea that

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.750
somebody spent time working on
building this thing and you're

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:13.510
going to get rid of it.

00:39:13.510 --> 00:39:15.790
And so we promise we're
not getting rid of it,

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:17.620
it will still exist
if you ever need it.

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:19.800
I think that's the safe
thing even though, really,

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:21.800
some of these are
never coming back.

00:39:24.740 --> 00:39:26.450
- George, I see you have
your hand raised here too.

00:39:26.450 --> 00:39:28.190
You want to jump in?

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:31.340
- Yeah, we actually did that

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:32.810
with the dashboard

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:34.040
because we actually--

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:36.650
it was a Power BI dashboard
and it tells you when--

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:38.760
it gives us reports
that no one's using it.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.845
And so we decided to
stop updating it and found that

00:39:42.845 --> 00:39:47.090
OK, they actually did need
it, but not as frequently.

00:39:47.090 --> 00:39:49.640
And so I think just going
back to the requirements for

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:52.520
building it and understanding

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:54.660
the frequency of that.

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:57.590
And so just having a
discussion about what were the

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:59.840
expectations. So realizing, oh,

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:02.300
they really only need
like once a quarter.

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:03.260
But

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:07.760
we built it in a way that
could be done every day,

00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:10.085
but we didn't need to
update the data every day.

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:14.840
So sometimes,

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:17.360
it's just understanding the

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:20.460
requirements and not
necessarily going to think,

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:23.330
well, they don't ever use
it. Well, when they use it,

00:40:23.330 --> 00:40:25.200
it's super important.

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:27.200
It's just not frequent.

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:30.050
Yeah.

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:32.480
- Absolutely. I

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:36.440
see all this fun chat happening
during the hangout too.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:38.870
And I am jealous I
don't get to read it.

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:41.510
But I'm going to read
that all after too.

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:44.900
I love to see the party
happening there. But there was

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:49.190
a Slido question someone had
asked anonymously. I try to

00:40:49.190 --> 00:40:53.600
save code tips that I can
see uses for in later projects.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:55.430
But I feel like it's so messy.

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:59.480
How do you all keep track
of frequently used code, notes, and tips?

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:06.510
And Marcos, I see your

00:41:06.510 --> 00:41:08.550
hand raised here.

00:41:08.550 --> 00:41:08.820
MARCOS: Sorry,

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:11.760
I didn't think you'd see it
in time before someone else start talking.

00:41:11.760 --> 00:41:15.150
So I work in Python almost
exclusively at work.

00:41:15.150 --> 00:41:18.000
And I think I
mentioned that before.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:20.430
And we do a lot
of Jupyter Notebooks for interactive

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:22.380
things which I know people
have a lot of feelings about.

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:25.620
But I am determined to put as
much code as I can into GitHub.

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:26.910
So we have

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:28.890
GitHub repositories
for our main

00:41:28.890 --> 00:41:32.490
production things that are
controlled. Our team has--

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:35.670
my big team, my big
pricing team has a

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:37.260
repository that's
basically scripts.

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.000
And some of the scripts
can be things that we run

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:40.740
automatically. But some
of them are just like--

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:42.480
it's just a repository
that anyone can push to.

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:44.520
And so if I write anything that
I think is cool and that I want

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:47.280
to use later and I think
anyone else will have any benefit of,

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:49.800
I will put it into a utility
scripts folder in there.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:52.330
And it's version controlled and

00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:53.880
I'll tell people about it
and then other people sometimes

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:55.150
will contribute to it.

00:41:55.150 --> 00:41:58.560
And then I also have my
own little secret, private Marcos repo

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:01.440
where if something isn't quite
baked enough for to share with

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:03.610
everyone, I will save
it to my own repository.

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:05.850
And so version control

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:08.610
and GitHub, if you have
access to those tools,

00:42:08.610 --> 00:42:12.420
are a really good way to not lose

00:42:12.420 --> 00:42:15.910
stuff into some one off file or
some off off Jupyter Notebook or whatever.

00:42:15.910 --> 00:42:19.350
And so my strategy is to write
as much as I can inside an

00:42:19.350 --> 00:42:21.750
editor and save it
into a Git control

00:42:21.750 --> 00:42:23.850
code file. And then if I
need to use it in a notebook,

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:26.160
I will import it into my
notebook which you can do with

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:28.230
Python pretty easily.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:32.530
That I'm not exactly sure how
the other folks think about this

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:35.460
since I was there actually
when I was terrible at writing

00:42:35.460 --> 00:42:38.520
functions. But

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:40.340
that's kind of my strategy.

00:42:40.340 --> 00:42:45.210
And so if you haven't learned
about Git and version control, highly recommend

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:48.840
either chatting with
other folks here or

00:42:48.840 --> 00:42:52.380
just trying to
learn more about it.

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:54.300
- Definitely.

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:58.590
One resources I know
is the happy Git with R

00:42:58.590 --> 00:43:01.710
book, if anybody wanted to
share that in the chat. But

00:43:01.710 --> 00:43:04.510
Eugene, I see you
had your hand raised.

00:43:04.510 --> 00:43:07.650
EUGENE: Yeah. Functions,
functions, functions, functions--

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:11.820
just fictionalize it, give it
a descriptive name, put it in

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:16.740
somewhere that I created
a company R repo

00:43:16.740 --> 00:43:18.660
or R package.

00:43:18.660 --> 00:43:21.420
Just titled it the company
name. Everybody had a copy of

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:22.860
it, everybody could make edits,

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:24.690
everybody could
update those edits,

00:43:24.690 --> 00:43:26.490
and every single function was--

00:43:26.490 --> 00:43:29.580
it just became more and
more generalized which is the best

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:32.580
thing about writing functions
and forcing that everybody

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:33.420
writes functions.

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:35.850
And you can't finish writing
your function unless you

00:43:35.850 --> 00:43:39.120
actually create a manual page,
which is the best part about

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:44.265
it. So I know it takes an
extra 10 minutes and it's such a hassle

00:43:44.265 --> 00:43:47.640
to add that three
sentences of documentation that described

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:49.750
the arguments and what
you want to use it for.

00:43:49.750 --> 00:43:50.670
But I mean,

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:54.570
the overall build of
productivity over time is just--

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:57.000
nobody has to resolve
the same problems.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:00.660
We all have to convert dates,
we all have to convert zip

00:44:00.660 --> 00:44:02.910
codes, we all have
to add leading zeros,

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:06.840
we all convert things from
factors to strings or strings

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:09.420
to factors, et cetera,
count the number of factors.

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:11.460
Just build functions
that do this stuff.

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:13.420
Just build them and
leave them there.

00:44:13.420 --> 00:44:16.020
And any time anybody
has a problem like this,

00:44:16.020 --> 00:44:17.740
in your team meeting,
you say, hey,

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:19.890
what new functions were
added this past week.

00:44:19.890 --> 00:44:22.590
It's very simple to
get that sort of--

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:26.250
it's exponential productivity that
you'll get across the team.

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:34.530
- Do we need a Data
Science Hangout GitHub and packages? [LAUGHS]

