00:45:13.020 --> 00:45:18.540
Even if you're just doing it
for you, it's still a good practice.

00:45:18.540 --> 00:45:22.080
And then if you do onboard
more people and you start sharing--

00:45:22.080 --> 00:45:24.840
you can share that script
with them and then eventually make

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:26.200
that business case like, hey,

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:29.100
we do need a GitLab license
so that we can share these things

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:32.340
as our team grows and as
our organization grows.

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:34.125
And if it does
still just be you,

00:45:34.125 --> 00:45:36.125
you've made your
own life better.

00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:39.880
- Of course, yeah.

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:42.370
Thank you all for the
thoughts here too.

00:45:42.370 --> 00:45:46.350
Before leaving this topic,
I was kind of reminding

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:46.890
myself--

00:45:46.890 --> 00:45:48.420
I think Mike Smith,

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:51.615
you talked about this
by using snippets too?

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:55.050
- Yeah,

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:57.660
snippets are fabulous

00:45:57.660 --> 00:46:00.270
because you can give
a little shortcut

00:46:00.270 --> 00:46:03.630
and then it gives you
a skeleton or a framework

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:06.480
where you can jump into
certain parts within that framework and

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:08.805
kind of almost also
complete certain sections.

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:15.360
You can share snippets.
There's use this

00:46:15.360 --> 00:46:19.380
has a way of opening up the
snippet so that you can edit them.

00:46:23.220 --> 00:46:26.640
So for things that you
do frequently and where you

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:30.280
actually have that framework,
they're really, really helpful.

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:34.020
But I think I'm leaning
more towards what's just been said

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:36.020
about write functions

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:41.400
because it's like you can
also then document those, test them,

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.400
you can roll them
up into a package.

00:46:45.390 --> 00:46:47.670
- For some reason,
writing a function

00:46:47.670 --> 00:46:50.820
just sounds scarier to
me than the word snippet.

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:51.090
- Well,

00:46:51.090 --> 00:46:53.580
it does because writing a
function implies that you're

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:54.870
going to write
the documentation,

00:46:54.870 --> 00:46:57.850
then you're going to
maintain it forever,

00:46:57.850 --> 00:46:58.750
whereas a snippet--

00:46:58.750 --> 00:47:00.910
well, it's a bit
more throwaway.

00:47:04.890 --> 00:47:08.110
- Yeah, I think just a
point to add to this--

00:47:08.110 --> 00:47:10.500
this is something that I tried
to do in my company is also

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:12.850
sharing that you've
done this with others,

00:47:12.850 --> 00:47:14.820
making sure it's on
your knowledge based solution

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:15.900
because then you find out, oh,

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:17.640
this has already been
done by somebody else,

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:20.680
and I didn't have to put all
this effort if they'd only share it.

00:47:20.680 --> 00:47:23.160
So I think contributing to
the community is important

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:25.350
internally just as well
as it is externally.

