00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:21.070
- Let me go back to see
some questions we maybe

00:48:21.070 --> 00:48:23.680
had missed.

00:48:23.680 --> 00:48:27.865
Libby, I saw earlier you
had a question around collaboration.

00:48:27.865 --> 00:48:30.710
Do you want ask that one?

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:33.940
- It was probably about time.

00:48:33.940 --> 00:48:37.780
There's a conversation around
we really want people to

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:40.060
collaborate and how do we get
them to talk to each other and

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:43.600
how do we get them to
form a community and be more connected

00:48:43.600 --> 00:48:46.310
because we're all remote.
And my question is,

00:48:46.310 --> 00:48:49.600
do they have the time to
do that because if they don't,

00:48:49.600 --> 00:48:53.330
you are expecting something magical.
It's not going to happen.

00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:56.500
And so the push that

00:48:56.500 --> 00:49:01.350
I am trying to go
for is prioritize

00:49:01.350 --> 00:49:05.100
timing, prioritize chunks of
people's time for that stuff.

00:49:05.100 --> 00:49:07.350
If you don't tell them to do
it and that you want them to do

00:49:07.350 --> 00:49:10.080
it, give them the
time to do it.

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:13.290
It's probably not going to happen
because they're going to be heads down

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:15.180
and feeling too pressured.

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:17.940
And I was thinking about
there is a Harvard Business

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:22.110
Review article that I read
recently about capacity, about how people

00:49:22.110 --> 00:49:25.320
do their best work when
they're actually about 85%

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:30.860
capacity or 80%
capacity, not 100%. And

00:49:30.860 --> 00:49:33.890
aiming for people
to be at 80% or 85%

00:49:33.890 --> 00:49:36.890
does free them up a little
bit to have the mental and

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.940
emotional capacity to connect with
other human beings to share stuff.

00:49:40.940 --> 00:49:42.500
And

00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:45.260
we have all this double work
and we have all these silos.

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:46.820
Guess what? If people
talk to each other,

00:49:46.820 --> 00:49:48.230
those walls start
to break down.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:51.590
And you start to realize
other people are duplicating work.

00:49:51.590 --> 00:49:53.880
And you start to realize
what's possible because someone says,

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:55.850
oh, yeah I did that
in this other project.

00:49:55.850 --> 00:49:57.590
And maybe it's not
the exact same thing,

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:00.380
but it's going to help. So

00:50:00.380 --> 00:50:04.260
that's my two cents, I guess.
Less of the question, but

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:08.450
are you prioritizing your
people having that time.

00:50:08.450 --> 00:50:10.040
- I love that.
Thank you, Libby.

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:13.370
And I think Mike has
some thoughts to share on it.

00:50:13.370 --> 00:50:16.400
- Sorry, for being the guy
that jumps in here. But--

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:18.560
- You don't have to be sorry.

00:50:18.560 --> 00:50:24.200
- I've been the R guy at
our company for many, many, many years.

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:29.720
And before we really go
our R community going,

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:31.010
everyone came to me--

00:50:31.010 --> 00:50:33.050
oh, Michael know the answer
to this, oh, Michael,

00:50:33.050 --> 00:50:35.450
help me to solve out these
insulation issues, oh, Michael,

00:50:35.450 --> 00:50:39.600
help me answer this tricky question I've
got about how to do this thing.

00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:42.200
So and

00:50:42.200 --> 00:50:46.250
that then funnels
everyone in through me.

00:50:46.250 --> 00:50:48.740
And you might argue that
that's great for my brand.

00:50:48.740 --> 00:50:52.670
But it's making me beyond 100%
capacity and it's hard to keep

00:50:52.670 --> 00:50:53.840
up with all the request.

00:50:53.840 --> 00:50:57.170
And it means that I have
the same question again and again

00:50:57.170 --> 00:50:58.340
and again and again and again.

00:50:58.340 --> 00:51:01.280
And it gets really tedious
because even though I bang up

00:51:01.280 --> 00:51:03.920
to it and go, you
must do it this way,

00:51:03.920 --> 00:51:06.860
everyone's so busy
and they're like this

00:51:06.860 --> 00:51:08.180
and it never gets through.

00:51:08.180 --> 00:51:11.030
Once we have the
community in place,

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:14.960
we have a single source of here's
the information and how to install R,

00:51:14.960 --> 00:51:19.700
here's the information on how
to get the packages you need.

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:23.720
We have that place for the
written documentation, we have

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:27.230
our community sessions that
say, if anyone's got questions,

00:51:27.230 --> 00:51:29.030
they come in through
that community page.

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:32.090
And anyone can
answer that question.

00:51:32.090 --> 00:51:33.590
So

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:36.950
it's absolutely vital

00:51:36.950 --> 00:51:39.710
that the community gets
going because otherwise,

00:51:39.710 --> 00:51:44.450
it all funnels through your
regional local friendly R expert.

00:51:44.450 --> 00:51:46.700
And that person just
winds up burning out.

00:51:48.850 --> 00:51:51.310
So we've

00:51:51.310 --> 00:51:54.910
been talking about the ROI
on taking time to build community.

00:51:54.910 --> 00:51:57.430
But without it,

00:51:57.430 --> 00:52:00.040
someone's going to burn out
because they're at the sharp

00:52:00.040 --> 00:52:02.040
end of that funnel.

00:52:03.090 --> 00:52:03.870
Sorry,

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:06.930
voices of experience and also
feeling thankful that my SWAT

00:52:06.930 --> 00:52:10.400
team is there and we're
building community every day. So

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:12.870
make up the SWAT team.

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:16.260
- That's great. Yeah, big

00:52:16.260 --> 00:52:20.640
kudos to the Pfizer team
