00:55:18.140 --> 00:55:21.250
they had certain functions that
had certain edits for them for

00:55:21.250 --> 00:55:23.740
one of these particular
use cases. I mean,

00:55:23.740 --> 00:55:27.100
it was terrible in terms
of overall productivity and

00:55:27.100 --> 00:55:29.680
building models took
literally three days.

00:55:29.680 --> 00:55:32.620
And it took me about
six months to a year.

00:55:32.620 --> 00:55:33.520
But in a year,

00:55:33.520 --> 00:55:38.380
I had people building models in
four hours instead of three days.

00:55:38.380 --> 00:55:42.610
And we were all using the exact
same R version and we were all

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:47.560
using snippets of functions that
slowly got better because somebody finally

00:55:47.560 --> 00:55:48.910
put one into a package.

00:55:48.910 --> 00:55:52.090
And when somebody checked out
that function and actually did

00:55:52.090 --> 00:55:52.810
something with it,

00:55:52.810 --> 00:55:55.030
it broke for them because
they were using a slightly

00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:58.120
alternative use case. So
what did we teach them to do?

00:55:58.120 --> 00:56:01.330
You add a new argument.
And then having that new argument,

00:56:01.330 --> 00:56:03.010
you add that to
the documentation.

00:56:03.010 --> 00:56:05.740
And then on the bottom,
you have these edits.

00:56:05.740 --> 00:56:07.900
You just add an
if/else statement

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:09.370
based on that new argument.

00:56:09.370 --> 00:56:12.760
And now the function does
both things for both people.

00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:16.720
And this literally began
the collaborative process.

00:56:16.720 --> 00:56:20.200
And there were people who
took two years to finally check-in

00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:22.450
their own brand new function
even though they were making

00:56:22.450 --> 00:56:26.500
edits of functions in the meantime.
And it was a big, yay,

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:29.200
Amanda finally checked in
her first function, wohoo!

00:56:29.200 --> 00:56:34.450
And the package grew and more
use cases were handled and our

00:56:34.450 --> 00:56:37.270
entire library of
code got better

00:56:37.270 --> 00:56:38.920
and our ability to
talk to each other--

00:56:38.920 --> 00:56:40.870
hey, didn't you build
something that did that,

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:44.110
didn't you build something
that solved a similar problem.

00:56:44.110 --> 00:56:47.590
And if it doesn't do the
exact same thing you do,

00:56:47.590 --> 00:56:51.160
you get to be the unit
tester and figure out how it works.

00:56:51.160 --> 00:56:53.020
And then you build
experience with Git.

00:56:53.020 --> 00:56:55.900
Now your resume grows
because you have experience working

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.990
with repos, you have
experience working with version control,

00:56:58.990 --> 00:57:02.530
you have experience modifying
functions, automating your work.

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:03.820
All these things are like--

00:57:03.820 --> 00:57:05.890
you're solving the
calibration problem,

00:57:05.890 --> 00:57:09.550
you're solving a productivity
problem, you're solving

00:57:09.550 --> 00:57:12.730
the ability to actually
contribute and build the skill

00:57:12.730 --> 00:57:15.770
sets that every future job is
going to need from you. I mean,

00:57:15.770 --> 00:57:17.560
the whole thing is
literally like--

00:57:17.560 --> 00:57:19.300
you can solve, in my opinion,

00:57:19.300 --> 00:57:22.180
multiple problems just by
trying to centralize your code

00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:25.510
and get everybody to
work together on similar code,

00:57:25.510 --> 00:57:28.060
similar projects.

00:57:28.060 --> 00:57:29.350
And it doesn't even
have to be similar.

00:57:29.350 --> 00:57:35.170
But we're all data scientists and
we have to be like wild people,

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:36.790
sometimes writing SQL parsers.

00:57:36.790 --> 00:57:39.010
And then suddenly, you can
talk to the database people,

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:41.650
and they build a scripting
function that builds a SQL

00:57:41.650 --> 00:57:45.490
parser build SQL queries for
them with a few arguments. And

00:57:45.490 --> 00:57:49.540
we had that experience of being
able to finally hand off SQL

00:57:49.540 --> 00:57:54.100
queries or SQL query builders
to our own business analysts

00:57:54.100 --> 00:57:58.750
that worked in SQL all day.
And yes, we used to R staple

00:57:58.750 --> 00:58:02.290
all this text together and
build SQL queries and they were

00:58:02.290 --> 00:58:03.250
just amazed. They
were like, oh,

00:58:03.250 --> 00:58:05.890
this is how you guys get
that done so fast. Yes,

00:58:05.890 --> 00:58:08.608
and you can do it too.

00:58:08.608 --> 00:58:10.190
- I love that.

00:58:10.190 --> 00:58:13.210
I love how passionate you are
about it, but also that, yes,

00:58:13.210 --> 00:58:15.520
and you can do it too.

00:58:15.520 --> 00:58:18.500
One last question
I wanted to ask.

00:58:18.500 --> 00:58:21.700
I see Priyanka asked a question
that is kind of on the same

00:58:21.700 --> 00:58:24.730
topic around making
time for community,

00:58:24.730 --> 00:58:27.280
but for the team builders
and leaders here.

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:31.300
How do you encourage your
teams to keep upskilling while

00:58:31.300 --> 00:58:33.300
continuing the
business as usual?

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:40.250
- I'm really lucky. I have
Sam Palmer on the team.

00:58:40.250 --> 00:58:42.830
And he contributes to R weekly.

00:58:42.830 --> 00:58:44.780
And so

00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:47.660
I see that as part
of his job too.

00:58:47.660 --> 00:58:51.620
It used to be a hobby, now
it's actually part of his job. But Sam

00:58:51.620 --> 00:58:54.890
collects that information
anyway. But then internally,

00:58:54.890 --> 00:58:59.240
they produce a little kind
of monthly blog post to see

00:58:59.240 --> 00:59:01.020
what are we seeing as
the latest greatest.

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:03.020
And that helps enormously.

00:59:06.410 --> 00:59:10.160
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's
great. Any other thoughts

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:14.280
in encouraging your
teams to keep upskilling?

00:59:14.280 --> 00:59:14.355
-

00:59:14.355 --> 00:59:16.620
I'm going to say the same
thing that I did for community stuff,

00:59:16.620 --> 00:59:20.100
which is put that
time into people's

00:59:20.100 --> 00:59:21.990
reviews and into their weeks.

00:59:21.990 --> 00:59:25.260
I've had jobs where it's
expected that 10% of my time is

00:59:25.260 --> 00:59:27.190
upskilling, and I need to
be able to talk about it.

00:59:27.190 --> 00:59:28.950
I need to be able
to say, this month,

00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:31.920
I used 10% of my time
to do X, Y and Z.

00:59:31.920 --> 00:59:34.740
And if it's a part of the
accountability for somebody,

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:37.710
then they know that
they're allowed to do it.

00:59:37.710 --> 00:59:39.710
- Yeah.

00:59:43.340 --> 00:59:44.900
- It's hard to write--

00:59:44.900 --> 00:59:49.010
back to also what
was said earlier that

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:52.940
your focus on your day job
to get stuff done and off your

00:59:52.940 --> 00:59:54.860
desk and out the door

00:59:54.860 --> 00:59:57.620
means that it's hard to
assign time to learn.

00:59:57.620 --> 01:00:01.730
I was just thinking there that
we have people who learned base R

01:00:01.730 --> 01:00:05.420
15 years ago and they haven't
even got to the tidyverse yet.

01:00:05.420 --> 01:00:07.790
And then we've got other
people who learned the tidyverse five

01:00:07.790 --> 01:00:11.960
years ago and
aren't aware of the

01:00:11.960 --> 01:00:15.880
most recent changes
to functions.

01:00:15.880 --> 01:00:17.710
And so you've

01:00:17.710 --> 01:00:24.130
got to keep that
ball rolling and continuous improvement

01:00:24.130 --> 01:00:26.800
and just kind of keep chucking
it under people's noses.

01:00:30.410 --> 01:00:34.010
- I like the ideas of
actually putting it on your calendar.

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:36.380
I see, Tony, you mentioned

01:00:36.380 --> 01:00:39.650
you're encouraged to use
two hours per week upskilling.

01:00:42.640 --> 01:00:45.220
I've been thinking
about this with--

01:00:45.220 --> 01:00:48.310
I talked with our
president yesterday about sometimes, I

01:00:48.310 --> 01:00:51.190
have a tendency to want to
focus on a lot of things and a

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:53.980
lot of different ideas
and making things better.

01:00:53.980 --> 01:00:56.410
But it's also important to

01:00:56.410 --> 01:00:59.740
pick what is the
most important thing

01:00:59.740 --> 01:01:06.390
that I want to focus on and
trying to block a whole day for that

01:01:06.390 --> 01:01:08.820
just as dedicated focus time.

01:01:08.820 --> 01:01:10.140
- Tony, I think--

01:01:10.140 --> 01:01:12.720
do you want to add
something there?

01:01:12.720 --> 01:01:14.250
- Yes,

01:01:14.250 --> 01:01:15.720
it doesn't always happen.

01:01:15.720 --> 01:01:22.140
But if you can have that goal
and maybe put two hours on your calendar,

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:25.320
especially if you have

01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:27.960
a remote work day

01:01:27.960 --> 01:01:32.460
and can avoid having
meetings during that time.

01:01:32.460 --> 01:01:34.550
A lot of times,

01:01:34.550 --> 01:01:38.390
it seems hard to do because
of the immediate needs,

01:01:38.390 --> 01:01:41.480
the fires that you
have to put out.

01:01:41.480 --> 01:01:43.760
But

01:01:43.760 --> 01:01:47.780
it can pay off in the long run
because something could take a lot less time

01:01:49.790 --> 01:01:52.850
because you've already got
that baseline of knowledge on

01:01:52.850 --> 01:01:55.340
whatever skill it
is you need. So

01:01:55.340 --> 01:01:59.210
it's definitely worth it if you can find
a way to fit it into your schedule.

01:02:01.410 --> 01:02:04.950
- Definitely. And George, I
see your hand's raised too.

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:07.650
- Yeah, I wanted
to share my latest

01:02:07.650 --> 01:02:10.020
tip or trick for

01:02:10.020 --> 01:02:17.340
upskilling. I'm going back to the old
days of using RSS feeds in my Outlook.

01:02:17.340 --> 01:02:19.980
And something that

01:02:19.980 --> 01:02:21.750
you used to see back in

01:02:21.750 --> 01:02:27.690
a long time ago, '90s and early
2000s, so now I get our bloggers

01:02:27.690 --> 01:02:33.210
and a lot of other
resources sent directly to my Outlook

01:02:33.210 --> 01:02:35.280
application. And

01:02:35.280 --> 01:02:38.160
every time you see that
little number saying how many unread

01:02:38.160 --> 01:02:41.310
messages you have, I know like,
OK, there's another article

01:02:41.310 --> 01:02:44.980
I'm going to keep up with.
And that's been really,

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:46.980
really helpful for me lately.

01:02:47.990 --> 01:02:49.730
- That's a great tip. Well,

01:02:49.730 --> 01:02:52.940
I know we are a
little bit over on our

01:02:52.940 --> 01:02:57.410
time here. But thank you all
so much for jumping in the

01:02:57.410 --> 01:03:01.110
conversation today and being
the featured leaders here.

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:02.990
I love to see all of this.

01:03:02.990 --> 01:03:04.530
The chat happening as well.

01:03:04.530 --> 01:03:06.950
And I'm excited to
go read that later.

01:03:06.950 --> 01:03:10.950
But thank you all again so
much for joining us here today.

01:03:10.950 --> 01:03:14.630
And I wanted to let everybody
know that next week,

01:03:14.630 --> 01:03:18.380
Joe Chang, our CTO at
Posit, will be our featured leader.

01:03:18.380 --> 01:03:21.110
Joe is the creator of Shiny.

01:03:21.110 --> 01:03:23.750
And that will be the

01:03:23.750 --> 01:03:25.560
